\ PATHSET.SEQ   Words used to set the path of a file.   by Tom Zimmer
\                                                       F-PC native port

\ PDOS (DOS 47h) returns the host's current directory without a leading
\ slash and with \ separators, so "C:\home\me\dir\FILE.SEQ" results; the
\ VM drops the drive and maps it back to /home/me/dir/FILE.SEQ. A name
\ starting with \ or / is left alone. Sizes come from B/FILENAME.

   0 value flhndl            \ plugged later

: ?drive.extract ( handle --- drive-value )
                dup >nam 1+ c@ ':' =
                if      dup>r >nam c@ bl or 96 -
                        r@ count 2- >r dup 2+ swap r> cmove
                        r@ c@ 2- r@ c! r> count + off
                else    drop 0 25 bdos 1+ then    ;

: ?drive.prepend ( drive-value handle --- )
                over 0=
                if      2drop
                else    dup>r count >r dup 2+ r> cmove>
                        64 + r@ >nam c! ':' r@ >nam 1+ c!
                        r@ c@ 2+ r> c!
                then    ;

\- pathhndl     handle pathhndl

: prepend.path  ( handle --- f1 )
                dup c@ 0= if drop true exit then    \ leave if no name
                pathhndl clr-hcb
                dup =: flhndl ?drive.extract >r
                flhndl >nam c@ dup '\' <> swap '/' <> and   \ leave if got path
        if      '\' pathhndl 1+ c!
                pathhndl 2+ r@ pdos 0=
                if      pathhndl 1+ dup 255 0 scan drop \ determine path length
                        swap - 255 min pathhndl c!
                        pathhndl c@ 1 >
                        if      '\' pathhndl count + c!
                                pathhndl c@ 1+ pathhndl c!
                        then
                        \ Native port: if the result would not fit in the
                        \ handle (host directories are deep), or PDOS may
                        \ have cut the directory short (127 chars), leave
                        \ the name relative: the host resolves it against
                        \ the same current directory.
                        flhndl c@ pathhndl c@ + b/filename 3 - > 0=
                        pathhndl c@ 128 < and
                        if      flhndl 1+ pathhndl c@ over + flhndl c@ cmove>
                                pathhndl count flhndl 1+ swap cmove
                                pathhndl c@ flhndl c@ + flhndl c!
                        else    r>drop 0 >r     \ and no drive either
                        then
                then
        then    r> flhndl ?drive.prepend 0 flhndl count + c! false ;

' PREPEND.PATH IS PATHSET

